## Core Challenge — Visual Confusion at 96×96

The fundamental blocker across all runs is that at 96×96 pixels:

- A **metal** can (bare aluminium, silver/grey) and a **recyclable** can (same shape, coloured paper label) occupy ~20×50 pixels — barely distinguishable
- **General** trash (items in clear bags) overlaps visually with **recyclable** (also items in bags)
- The **GAP head** collapses the entire 12×12×128 feature map into a single 128-dim vector — no spatial texture cues survive

The two dominant error pairs across all runs: `general ↔ recyclable` and `metal ↔ recyclable`.

---

## What Needs to Happen to Hit the Target

Gaps are small in absolute terms:

| Class | Current | Target | Gap | Images needed |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| General | 0.645 | 0.80 | +0.155 | +6 correct out of 31 test images |
| Recyclable | 0.625 | 0.80 | +0.175 | +8 correct out of 32 test images |
| Metal | 0.656 | 0.80 | +0.144 | +5 correct out of 32 test images |
| Biodegradable | 0.848 | 0.80 | — | Already passing ✅ |

### Paths Forward

1. **Spatial FOMO head** *(recommended first try)* — Remove GAP, keep the 12×12×128 feature map, apply a 3×3 depthwise conv before the head. Local texture patches can separate `recyclable` (label texture) from `metal` (smooth reflective surface). No new data needed, minimal size increase.

2. **Knowledge distillation** — Train a larger teacher at 192×192 where the classes are visually distinct. Use its soft probability outputs to supervise the 96×96 student. The student inherits class separation it cannot learn directly.

3. **Dataset cleaning** — The Roboflow dataset contains mislabelled and ambiguous images. Removing irreducibly ambiguous samples reduces the noise floor that no architecture can overcome.

4. **QAT (Quantization-Aware Training)** — Float32 ≈ INT8 results across all 19 runs, so quantisation is not the bottleneck. QAT is a last-resort polish, not the primary fix.

